    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "The evolution of cement sales (or any data series) over time is rarely random. It often reflects underlying patterns - a story of the past, present and future. \n",
    " \n",
    "Time series analysis is about uncovering these patterns and using them to make informed predictions about the future.\n",
    "   \n",
    "# 📊 Time-Series Analysis \n",
    "    \n",
    "## 🔍 Looking Inside a Time Series: Decomposition\n",
    "    \n",
    "A time series is rarely just a collection of random observations.\n",
    "    \n",
    "When we observe a variable over time, its movements may reflect several underlying patterns.\n",
    "  \n",
    "For our **quarterly cement sales**, we might observe:\n",
    "  \n",
    "- 📈 a long-term increase in sales,\n",
    "- 🔄 a recurring quarterly pattern,  \n",
    "- 🌊 longer-term fluctuations, and  \n",
    "- ⚡ unexpected shocks.  \n",
    "  \n",
    "**Time-series decomposition** gives us a way to separate these different sources of variation.\n",
    "  \n",
    "> 💡 **Think of decomposition as taking a complicated time series apart to understand what is happening inside it.**\n",
    "    \n",
    "### 🧩 The Four Components of a Time Series\n",
    "  \n",
    "| Component | Icon | The question we are asking |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **Trend** | 📈 | Is the series moving upward or downward over the long run? |\n",
    "| **Seasonality** | 🔄 | Does a pattern repeat at a regular frequency? |\n",
    "| **Cycle** | 🌊 | Are there longer-term rises and falls? |\n",
    "| **Irregular** | ⚡ | What unusual movements remain unexplained? |\n",
    "    \n",
    "## 🧠 The Big Picture\n",
    "\n",
    "Think of an observed time series as the result of several underlying patterns working together:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\\text{Observed} = \\text{Trend} + \\text{Seasonality} + \\text{Irregular}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The additive model assumes that the components contribute **independently in absolute terms**. For example, seasonal fluctuations remain roughly constant in size over time.\n",
    "\n",
